            <ArticleTitle>Computed Navigation Guidance for Ankle Replacement in the Setting of Ankle Deformity.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>85-94</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0891-8422(17)30085-X</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.cpm.2017.08.004</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Total ankle replacement (TAR) has evolved over the past decade as a treatment for end-stage ankle arthritis with improved survivorship. Despite the improving outcomes, ankle deformity represents a challenge to the foot and ankle surgeon with increased risk of implant failure. The use of preoperative computer-assisted guidance has led to better understanding the 3-dimensional ankle anatomy and associated deformities and allows for reproducible, anatomic placement of the TAR components.</AbstractText>
